PART II — SOFTWARE USAGE RIGHTS

6. AUTHORIZED USE

Authorized users may use the software within the scope of the license granted to them.

Permitted activities may include:

- Installing authorized versions.
- Accessing permitted application features.
- Operating the software for approved purposes.
- Using authorized integrations.
- Accessing documentation.
- Using supported functionality.
- Creating and managing authorized projects.

Any additional rights must be established through an applicable license or written agreement.

7. PROHIBITED ACTIVITIES

Without appropriate authorization, users must not:

1. Redistribute proprietary copies of the software.
2. Sell unauthorized copies.
3. Publish protected source code.
4. Remove applicable ownership notices.
5. Misrepresent themselves as the software owner.
6. Create unauthorized commercial replicas.
7. Circumvent access controls.
8. Extract confidential proprietary materials.
9. Distribute unauthorized modified versions.
10. Use the software in violation of applicable law.

These restrictions apply only to the extent permitted by applicable law and do not override rights granted by third-party licenses.

8. COPYING AND REPRODUCTION

8.1 Reproduction of protected proprietary materials requires authorization unless permitted by law.

8.2 Authorized backup copies may be made where permitted by the applicable license and law.

8.3 Distribution of copies to unauthorized parties is prohibited.

8.4 Any permitted reproduction must preserve required notices and attributions.

9. MODIFICATION AND CUSTOMIZATION

9.1 Modification of proprietary components requires the appropriate authorization where applicable.

9.2 Authorized developers may modify software within the scope of their development permissions.

9.3 Customization rights do not automatically transfer ownership of the underlying software.

9.4 Ownership of commissioned developments and independently created contributions must be established through applicable contracts and law.

9.5 Third-party components remain governed by their own licenses.

10. REVERSE ENGINEERING

To the extent permitted by applicable law, users must not reverse engineer, decompile, disassemble, or attempt to extract protected proprietary source code without authorization.

Nothing in this section restricts activities that cannot legally be prohibited, including applicable statutory interoperability rights.

